            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>



During the blower door test, I identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch in the closet which can be sealed with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack". The attic hatch in the hallway appeared not to have as much air leakage. 



** Around the small door in to the woodshed section of your addition, as well as around the exterior door to your addition. 



** There is also some air leakage at the unfinished bathroom on the second floor. Paying attention to the air sealing as you finish this renovation will affect your air leakage. Pay close attention to the connections between the floor and the walls, and the walls and the ceiling. 



** I noted some air leakage at the wood stove chimney in the living room. The fireplace chimney is also quite large and will be a source of air leakage. 



** I also noted some air leakage at electrical outlets and switches in the exterior walls of your home. This can be reduced by installing foam gaskets behind the face plates at the outlets and switches. 



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $300 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 9.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>



The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 with R12 batt insulation, with an additional 1 inch of R5 insulation added to the exterior for a total nominal R value of R17. I recommend you add a minimum R15 to the exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding. This will bring your walls up to approximately R30, which is in line with requirements for new construction.



The new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings: By adding insulation to your above foundation exterior walls as detailed above, you could potentially save $1,090 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 34.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA, and my visit on 6 January 2021 satisfies the requirement for the pre-retrofit evaluation. 



This program has been extended until 31 March 2024, pending available funding. If you are considering taking advantage of this program, please contact me for more information. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <windows>



All of the windows in your home except for the window in your laundry room and the window in your addition have already been upgraded to triple pane windows, which appear to have a low-E coating.  I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading these two windows as well as the frosted window next to the exterior door in the addition with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $80 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.6 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



This boiler also heats your water. 



I also recommend that you further investigate the "heat pump" switch next to the emergency boiler switch, as I was unable to find a heat pump while I was on site. 



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $1,850 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 35.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). You have already installed ECM circulation pumps on your hydronic heating system, which is in line with our recommendations. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ECM Pumps

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency. These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



You have already done this for your boiler system, but as you are on piped water, your water circulation pump could be replaced with an ECM pump. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Wood Stove

As you have an existing fireplace and have recently added a woodstove to your living room, I have modelled the savings for using each of these to meet 25% of your heating load. 



Usiong only your fireplace to meet 25% of the heating load, you can expect to save approximately $100 per year in energy costs. Using only your new woodstove to meet 25% of the heating load, you can expect to save approximately $175 per year in energy costs. Both these savings estimates include a number of assumptions, so your actual savings from using either of these appliances may vary. 



If you have not already applied for a rebate for the woodstove, woodstoves are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase cost up to $1,000 under the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 8.0 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your furnace is atmospherically vented, so it is susceptible to back drafting. 



I did see the combustion supply pipe into your utility room for your boiler, but as it was obstructed I was not able to confirm whether or not it has a damper. I suggest you confirm this, and continue to work with your heating contractor to ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your boiler are satisfied. The combustion air pipe could have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the boileris operating.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - four bathroom fans - including the bathroom which is currently under renovation

        - a clothes dryer

        - a range hood



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat, which you have already installed.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.21 per liter 

Propane - $0.683 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



